@* A passage of the book, set as the book sets it.

   **It replaced two hand-written copies of `.book-text`.** The Power editor's disclosure and the
   rules reference each looped their own paragraphs into their own div, and the reference's own
   comment already said the two should be one idiom. They are now, and `WebPresentationTests`
   owns the class — a third copy fails a test rather than drifting.

   **What it draws is what `RulebookProse` found, and that is only what the book marks.** A stat
   line, a generic option's price, and the Pros and Cons printed inside an entry. The description
   itself is left exactly as the extraction hands it over: the corpus runs a Power's entry together
   on one line, so a Power's description is still one run, and breaking it here on sentence count
   would be this component deciding where the author's paragraphs were. That gap is the
   extractor's — see PROGRESS.md — and pretending otherwise would put invented structure in front
   of a reader who cannot tell.

   The mechanics beside this come from `data/rules`, which is verified entry by entry. This is the
   book's wording next to them, never instead of them. *@

<div class="book-text">
    @if (Prose.Stat is { } stat)
    {
        @* Three labelled cells rather than one bullet run, because a reader coming to a Power's
           entry is looking up one of the three. The bullets are how the page fits them on one
           line; a screen has room to say which is which. *@
        <dl class="book-stat">
            <div><dt>Range</dt><dd>@stat.Range</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Rank</dt><dd>@stat.RankType</dd></div>
            <div><dt>Cost</dt><dd>@stat.Cost</dd></div>
        </dl>
    }

    @if (Prose.Lead is { } lead)
    {
        <p class="book-lead">@lead</p>
    }

    @foreach (var paragraph in Prose.Paragraphs)
    {
        <p>@paragraph</p>
    }

    @if (Prose.Options.Count > 0)
    {
        <ul class="book-options">
            @foreach (var option in Prose.Options)
            {
                <li>
                    <div class="head">
                        @* One expression, so the parts cannot be run together: Razor strips the
                           leading whitespace inside an element that follows an expression, which
                           is how "Armor8d" reached the sheet twice. *@
                        <span class="kind @option.Kind.ToLowerInvariant()">@option.Kind</span>
                        <b>@option.Name</b>
                        @* **Not `.hp`.** That class is for a Hero Point figure the engine
                           answered — smaller, letter-spaced and set in capitals — and a price the
                           book writes in words comes out of it as "+1 PER RANK". This is the
                           book's own phrase, so it is set like the lead: display face, muted, and
                           in the case it was printed in. *@
                        <span class="price">@option.Price</span>
                    </div>
                    <p>@option.Text</p>
                </li>
            }
        </ul>
    }

    @if (Citation is not null)
    {
        <p class="small muted">@Citation</p>
    }
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>The passage, verbatim, as the corpus holds it.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public string Text { get; set; } = "";

    /// <summary>
    /// Where it is printed, in the reader's own terms — "Ch.2 Characters, printed p.33".
    ///
    /// <para>Passed in rather than built here: the two call sites hold different records, and a
    /// citation assembled from whichever fields this component could see would be a second
    /// spelling of the one thing this text is worth more than memory for.</para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Citation { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Parsed once per change of passage, not once per render. It is three regular expressions
    /// over up to a few thousand characters, and this component is inside a list that redraws on
    /// every keystroke in the filter box above it.
    /// </summary>
    private RulebookProse Prose { get; set; } = RulebookProse.Read(null);

    protected override void OnParametersSet() => Prose = RulebookProse.Read(Text);
}
